Z||000002||htexans1||11-09-2001||09:21 PM||||Kansas Racing Parts company has all of this info on their web page. Do a search in yahoo and its address will show up<BR>S.Williams||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||11-09-2001||09:32 PM||||If I remember correctly you can use the stock head on an SD4 but there is some machine work that needs to be done. Also a chevy V-8 head works too. Kansas Racing Products uses the Chevy 2.02 heads.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cooter||11-09-2001||09:34 PM||||This might sound like a dumb question, but why would you want to use a stock crank in the superduty block? Isn't the crank part of the weakest link in the stock iron duck?||||reg||1
Z||000005||fieroman_5000||11-12-2001||08:06 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cooter:<BR><B>This might sound like a dumb question, but why would you want to use a stock crank in the superduty block? Isn't the crank part of the weakest link in the stock iron duck?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Good Question...it's just tmperary so i can gather the $ to get the SD crank.<P>Thanx for All the info guys!<P>Fieroman <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||14
Z||000006||Oreif||11-12-2001||08:15 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cooter:<BR><B>This might sound like a dumb question, but why would you want to use a stock crank in the superduty block? Isn't the crank part of the weakest link in the stock iron duck?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The crank isn't weak it's the rods. Also the SD block has 4-bolt main bearing caps and the stock block only uses 2-bolt mains. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||fieroman_5000||11-12-2001||08:29 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Oreif:<BR><B> The crank isn't weak it's the rods. Also the SD block has 4-bolt main bearing caps and the stock block only uses 2-bolt mains. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I'll be using the Chevy rods in it after much persuasion from dad. I will eventually build it up, I just need the $!!!Thanx<P>Fieroman  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||10
Z||000008||Jimmy||11-12-2001||08:12 PM||||I thought Kansas Racing had the SD4 block specially machined to use a SB head?  If he has an non Kansas Racing block I would think it would be pretty hard to use a SB head on it.<P>Jim||||reg||1
Z||000009||erikred||11-13-2001||10:07 AM||||If you use the stock crank, you will need to bush the mains. The SD crank is thicker, if I recall correctly. Consider using a Mercruiser crank, rods, and pistons. That will bump you up to 3.0l and the components are stronger. KRP sells these used at a good price.<P>The stock head should work fine.<P>BTW, if you come across another cheap SD block, I am interested!<P>Erik-||||reg||14
Z||000010||fieroman_5000||11-13-2001||03:04 PM||||There is this place up here in Michigan Flint actually that i'm getting mine from. It's going to cost $836.36 and I'm just going to pick it up. They also sell the SD head but it's $900+ You said to use the Mercruiser crank, rods, and pistons, are the rods shorter or what??? Cause from what I recall the 2.5L was a 3" bore and 4" stroke<P>Thanks for all the good info<P>Fieroman <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000011||erikred||11-13-2001||04:49 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fieroman_5000:<BR><B>There is this place up here in Michigan Flint actually that i'm getting mine from. It's going to cost $836.36 and I'm just going to pick it up. They also sell the SD head but it's $900+ You said to use the Mercruiser crank, rods, and pistons, are the rods shorter or what??? Cause from what I recall the 2.5L was a 3" bore and 4" stroke<P>Thanks for all the good info<P>Fieroman  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Is this a GM dealership you are buying from? Someone told me GM still sells the block, but I'll be damned if I can find it on their web site.<P>The Merc is 3.6" stroke and 4" bore. From what I am told the Merc parts work great in a stock Duke. You may have some minor adapting to do for the crank, but nothing too much. To find out for sure just call Gerry at KRP (785) 922-6644. He can answer all these questions for sure, and give you a good price on the parts.<P>Erik-<P>||||reg||1
Z||000012||fieroman_5000||11-13-2001||06:31 PM||||I did a search on these Mercruiser parts and all that came up were marine parts???? Nothing about cars at all. just a thought:P<BR>Would these pistons require boring the super duty block?<P>Fieroman <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000013||fieroman_5000||11-13-2001||09:03 PM||||It seems some of you are confused...i'm purchasing the Super Duty BLOCK from GM. This is possible as the GM dealer gets them through Kansas Racing Products. Now, I want my '84SE to have as little 'down time' as possible. I know it will be a while to complete as I need to  build the SD completely from scratch. This is the more expensive rout BUT the 2.7L Super Duty gets 205hp (normally asspirated) now thats something i really want.<BR>The Head will come and so will the crank and all that good stuff. I was just wondering whether I could recycle some of the stock parts if they are in a good condition. There. now you all know what my future planns are soooooo....what do you think???<P>Thanks again<P>Fieroman  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by fieroman_5000 (edited 11-13-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000014||Tryxalon||11-13-2001||10:04 PM||||IF you do pull it off?  Sweet! <P>||||reg||1
Z||000015||fieroman_5000||11-14-2001||08:04 AM||||I know how u feel...I'M Doing it!!!<BR>it'll be alot of work but iwant it ddone.  Any ideas on the amount of time needed to complete this project???<P>Fieroman <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000016||Raydar||11-14-2001||09:21 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fieroman_5000:<BR><B>I did a search on these Mercruiser parts and all that came up were marine parts???? Nothing about cars at all. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That's right. Marine parts are generally very heavy duty. Boat engines are designed to run at continuous high RPM. They also can over-rev if the prop comes out of the water for any reason, like jumping waves, etc. Hence, heavy-duty.<P>The MerCruiser makes 140 HP with a non-crossflow head. With a Super Duty head, it should make a bunch more. <P>Just my $.02.<P><P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000017||erikred||11-14-2001||09:54 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fieroman_5000:<BR><B>It seems some of you are confused...i'm purchasing the Super Duty BLOCK from GM. This is possible as the GM dealer gets them through Kansas Racing Products. Now, I want my '84SE to have as little 'down time' as possible. I know it will be a while to complete as I need to  build the SD completely from scratch. This is the more expensive rout BUT the 2.7L Super Duty gets 205hp (normally asspirated) now thats something i really want.<BR>The Head will come and so will the crank and all that good stuff. I was just wondering whether I could recycle some of the stock parts if they are in a good condition. There. now you all know what my future planns are soooooo....what do you think???<P>Thanks again<P>Fieroman   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>[This message has been edited by fieroman_5000 (edited 11-13-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You must be the person that told me about this in the first place. I have the hard copy HP catalog and have been all over the web site, but cannot find where I can order it through GM. I guess I will just have to call them. BTW, KRP sells raw unmachined blocks for $900, do they have some special deal where they sell them cheaper through GM?<P>Also, the Merc rods are 6" as are the stock ones the .6" difference must be in the pistons. If you go with Chevy rods you will need to work out the journal width difference. I am not sure what it is off the top of my head. I can look it up for you though. The Merc parts may work better for you, cheaper too.<P>Erik-<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||fieroman_5000||11-14-2001||10:18 AM||||sorry....double post<P><p>[This message has been edited by fieroman_5000 (edited 11-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||fieroman_5000||11-14-2001||10:22 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by erikred:<BR><B> You must be the person that told me about this in the first place. I have the hard copy HP catalog and have been all over the web site, but cannot find where I can order it through GM. I guess I will just have to call them. BTW, KRP sells raw unmachined blocks for $900, do they have some special deal where they sell them cheaper through GM?<P>Also, the Merc rods are 6" as are the stock ones the .6" difference must be in the pistons. If you go with Chevy rods you will need to work out the journal width difference. I am not sure what it is off the top of my head. I can look it up for you though. The Merc parts may work better for you, cheaper too.<P>Erik-</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thanks for the info on those merc parts!! I was looking for the cheapest part i could find, don't get me wrong...i don't want crap. BTW the block i'm geeting is NOT raw...it is the same ones that krp sells for$2500. Don't ask me why or how GM got them so cheap...i have no clue! Thaks for all that!<P>Fieroman <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||6
